## Slow-cooked dishes

**1. Tagine.** It takes its name from the conical clay pot in which it slowly simmers. There are many variations: chicken with preserved lemon and olives, kefta (minced meat balls) with eggs, lamb or beef with prunes and almonds, seasonal vegetables.

**2. Couscous.** Steamed semolina served with meat and vegetables, sometimes topped with sweet caramelised onions and raisins. In families, it is traditionally prepared on Fridays, the day of the main weekly prayer.

**3. Tanjia.** A Marrakech speciality, it cooks for many hours in a clay jar, traditionally placed in the embers of the fire that heats the neighbourhood hammam. The meat becomes meltingly tender, flavoured with cumin and preserved lemon.

## Festive specialities

**4. Pastilla.** A crisp pastry that combines sweet and savoury: poultry, almonds, eggs and cinnamon, dusted with icing sugar. There is also a seafood version.

**5. Mechoui.** Slow-roasted lamb, served on special occasions and in some specialist restaurants.

{% tip title="Table manners" %}
At a traditional meal, people often eat with the right hand, using a piece of bread. Watch your hosts and feel free to ask for cutlery.
{% /tip %}

## Soups and salads

**6. Harira.** A thick soup of tomatoes, lentils and chickpeas, flavoured with coriander and celery. It is closely linked to breaking the fast during Ramadan, but is available all year round.

**7. Cooked salads.** **Zaalouk** (spiced aubergine and tomato) and **taktouka** (peppers and tomatoes) often start the meal, served with bread.

## Breakfast and sweets

**8. Msemen and baghrir.** Msemen is a flaky pan-fried flatbread; baghrir is a "thousand-hole" pancake. They are served with honey and butter, for breakfast or as an afternoon snack.

**9. Pastries.** Gazelle horns filled with almond paste, chebakia with honey and sesame, almond ghriba: every pastry shop has its specialities.

**10. Mint tea.** Green tea, fresh mint and sugar, poured from a height to form a light foam. It accompanies meals, visits and negotiations.

## Eating well during your trip

- Choose busy places, where dishes are prepared and served quickly.
- If you have a sensitive stomach, favour well-cooked dishes served hot at the start of your stay.
- Drink sealed bottled water if you are unsure about the tap water.
- At markets, such as Jemaa el-Fna square in Marrakech, ask the price before ordering.

During Ramadan, the rhythm of meals changes: see [Travelling in Morocco during Ramadan](/en/guides/travelling-in-morocco-during-ramadan).
